# Knowledge Base programme

This rule applies **only** when the task is part of the Knowledge Base (KB) programme: the owner dragged a
`docs/SESSION-KB-*.md` file into the chat, or the request names a "KB lot". Ignore it otherwise.

**Authority.** The owner decided on 8 October 2026 to build the KB, which supersedes the "frozen until
14 October 2026" block of `v0-sprint.mdc` and the dated milestones of `AGENTS.md` for this work (lot 0
rewrites those files; until it is merged, this rule wins for KB work). Every other boundary stays: local
only, no cloud LLM, no telemetry, plan + approval for file actions, Compose environment, English for
developers and French for users, no real patient file.

**Before any code**, read in this order: `docs/SESSION-KB-WORKFLOW.md` (the ritual), `docs/SESSION-KB-00-master.md`
(invariants I1-I14, schema, decisions D1-D10), the section of your lot only, the reports of the lots
you depend on in `docs/test-reports/knowledge-base-pass-1/lots/`, and **the defects register**
(`docs/test-reports/knowledge-base-pass-1/defects-register.md`: every row not `fixed`, and every row that
names a file your lot touches). Do only your lot.

**Five duties at the end of every lot**, detailed in the workflow file:

1. **Branch.** Work on `feat/kb-<topic>` created from `kb/integration`. Never on `main`, never on
   `kb/integration` directly. Git writes are the owner's: hand over the full copy-paste command block.
2. **Document.** Write `docs/test-reports/knowledge-base-pass-1/lots/lot-NN-<slug>.md` (versioned) and
   update the docs the lot names. A lot with no written report is not done.
3. **Propose a real human test.** If the owner can see the lot working in the running application, you
   must propose a step-by-step UX/UI test (`human-tests/lot-NN-<slug>.md`) and ask her to run it. If only
   part of it is visible, propose that part. If nothing is visible, say so and offer the nearest developer
   check. Always include a non-regression smoke test. Never claim a screen works if only unit tests ran.
4. **Hand over.** Update `docs/SESSION-KB-STATUS.md` and name the next launcher file to drag into a new chat.
5. **Defects (owner instruction, 9 October 2026).** Record every defect you or the owner find, in
   `docs/test-reports/knowledge-base-pass-1/defects-register.md`, with its evidence, **the moment you see it**,
   whether or not the KB caused it. Analyse it against the lots still to come and write the effect: a defect
   that blocks, or will block, the KB is fixed **first**, in its own `fix/kb-<topic>` branch, and you stop and
   tell the owner; a defect that does not interfere is marked `deferred`, **not fixed in the middle of the
   programme**, and lot 11 proposes the targeted fix battery for all of them at the end. Do not fix a deferred
   defect on the side, and do not forget one: an unrecorded defect is a lost one.

**Exact commands in a human test (owner instruction, 11 October 2026, binding for every agent, in every lot).** The owner ran lot 5's test and lost time looking for commands that were abbreviated. Every step of a human test, in the protocol file **and** in the chat message, is complete and copy-pasteable:

1. **Every command is written in full**, from the repository root, with the exact path of the script and the exact name of the sub-command, for example `python docs\test-reports\knowledge-base-pass-1\human-tests\lot05_helper.py stop-ai`. Never `stop-ai` alone, never `lot05_helper.py ...`, never "the script".
2. **Every "in the script" or "look at the output" names the script and its arguments**, for example `python docs\test-reports\knowledge-base-pass-1\human-tests\show_kb_entities.py` or `show_kb_tables.py`. Say which of the two to run; if both are needed, say both.
3. **Every click is named by the exact label of the interface** (button, card, menu, tick box), and the card or screen it is on.
4. **Every expected result has been checked against what the application really does**, not against what the code should do: for example, with the AI machine stopped the application shows its own red connection banner (« Aucune réponse de l'IA ... ») whatever the analysis does; a test that says "no banner" there is wrong. If you cannot observe it, say so in the step instead of promising it.
5. **The chat message and the protocol file give the same steps in the same order with the same commands.** The chat message is the one the owner follows; it is never a summary of the file.
6. A command that must be typed has been run by the agent (on temporary folders) and its output is known; a name that is easy to mistype (`stop-ai`) is written out and copy-pasteable on its own line.
7. A step that stops or restarts a service gives the full command to put it back.
8. **Never ask the owner to run what the agent already ran (owner instruction, 11 October 2026, binding for every agent, in every lot and every fix).** `cargo test`, `cargo clippy`, `pnpm run test`, `pnpm run build`, `pytest` and any other automated suite are run by the agent, whose numbers go in the lot report ("Verification"); they are **not** a "Part A" of a human test, because their output is long and adds nothing she can check. A human test contains only what the agent cannot see or do: clicks and screens of the real application, the output of the read-only inspection scripts, answers to questions typed in the interface. The one exception is an automated command the owner must run **herself for an explicit reason** (for example a benchmark on her own machine, or a build that only her hardware can make); the step then states that reason in its first sentence.

No AI attribution in anything git-facing (`AGENTS.md`). Never run plain `cargo fmt`: format only the files
you touched with `rustfmt --edition 2021 <files>`.
